00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:54.550
ALEX BUTNAREANU: No worries.

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:55.500
We can handle that too.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:56.333
Let me show you how.

00:01:56.333 --> 00:01:57.450
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Sure.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.300
ALEX BUTNAREANU: Cloud KMS is
a hosted Key Management Service

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:02.550
that lets you manage
crypto keys for you

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:05.280
cloud services similar to the
way you'd do it on premise.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:08.550
It's also integrated into cloud
IM and cloud auto-logging,

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:10.770
so you can manage permissions
and individual keys

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:12.600
and monitor how they are used.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:15.390
We'll start by
creating a new keyring.

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:17.160
First, we name it.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.230
Then, we can choose a
location like make global.

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:21.330
However, the rank
location matters based

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:22.890
on where my resources are.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:25.830
I'm going to pick US central, as
that's where my workloads are.

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:27.820
Now I create my key.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:31.020
First, I can specify the name.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:34.480
Then, each key has
a purpose which

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:37.600
defines the cryptographic
capabilities of the key.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.510
I'm going to choose to use
this to do symmetric encrypt

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:41.830
or decrypt.

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:44.650
I can then choose if this is
software- or hardware-backed.

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:48.520
If there is a requirement to
use hardware, we can use HSM.

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:51.880
Cloud HSM is a Cloud Hosted
Hardware security module

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:55.210
service that allows you
to host encryption keys

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:56.920
and perform
cryptographic operations

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:01.900
in a cluster of Phipps 140-2
level 3 certified HSMs,

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:03.590
all managed by Google for you.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:07.250
Next, I can specify
the rotation period,

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:09.610
which is the time between
when the new key versions are

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:11.085
generated automatically.

00:03:11.085 --> 00:03:12.820
And let's say I want
to go ahead and use

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:16.810
hardware encryption via HSM,
and make the rotation period 30

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:19.830
days, and click "create."

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:22.315
Let's go back to our
storage bucket example

00:03:22.315 --> 00:03:23.940
I can create a new
bucket and configure

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:26.340
it to use my new
customer-managed encryption

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:28.080
key policies.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:30.690
I give it a name.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:36.940
Region is still US central
with my keys and HSMs,

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:40.390
and now I can change
the encryption options

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:44.410
from Google-managed key
to customee-managed key.

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:47.110
Soon as I do that, my
keyring and keys pop up,

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:50.690
and I can choose
which one to use.

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:52.690
Lastly, I need to
authorize a service account

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:55.660
to perform, encrypt,
and decrypt operations

00:03:55.660 --> 00:03:57.764
with the key I just selected.

00:03:57.764 --> 00:03:58.264
Granted.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.132
Now when I upload a
file, whether it's

00:04:04.132 --> 00:04:05.590
a few kilobytes or
a few petabytes,

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:07.720
it will be encrypted
using my key.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:13.080
So let's upload a file called
"Super Important File."

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:17.130
And you can see it's indeed
using customer-managed keys,

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.350
and it's the actual
key I selected.

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:22.450
When I click on
it, I can open it.

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:24.030
Now let's go back to my keys.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:26.350
The whole point
of CMEC using KMS

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:30.100
is to give customers
control over key policies.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:33.120
So let's say I want to go
and now disable that key.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.000
Once I disable it,
although not immediately,

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.550
I will no longer be
able to access the file.

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:40.950
Let's try it.

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:42.390
Software key's been disabled.

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:43.360
I try to open the file.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:46.410
I can see that I can no longer
access it, and the error I get

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:49.310
is that cloud KMS has been
disabled or destroyed.

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:52.080
And in the KMS UI, I can
see that the key I was using

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:53.710
was indeed disabled.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:56.160
That's what we mean by
customer-managed keys.

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:57.480
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Wow,
so now I have the option

00:04:57.480 --> 00:04:59.640
to choose what encryption
strategy makes sense for me,

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:01.932
and I can mix and match for
different parts of my cloud

00:05:01.932 --> 00:05:02.670
migration.

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:03.920
ALEX BUTNAREANU: That's right.

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:05.430
Encryption is a
complex topic, so we

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:08.190
work really hard to make
sure the full complexity is

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:09.240
abstracted away.

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:11.160
That way, you get
the best innovations

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:13.020
